<p>: ### Different types of losses we’ve seen before.</p>
<p>Mean Squared error loss:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AE%5B(%5Ctheta%20-%20%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%5E%7B2%7D%5D%20=%20Var(%5Ctheta%20-%20%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%20+%20E%5B(%5Ctheta%20-%20%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%5D%5E%7B2%7D%20%5C%5C%0A=%20Var(%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%20+%20E%5B(%5Ctheta%20-%20%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%5D%5E%7B2%7D%0A"></p>
<p>When <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D"> is <strong>unbiased,</strong> i.e., <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?E%5B%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D%5D%20=%20%5Ctheta"> then,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AMSE%20=%20E%5B(%5Ctheta%20-%20%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%5E%7B2%7D%5D%20=%20Var(%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D)%20+%200%0A"></p>
<p>Absolute Error Loss: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%7C%20%5Ctheta%20-%20%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D%7C"></p>
</section>
<section id="what-are-strategies-sx-estimators-hatthetax" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-are-strategies-sx-estimators-hatthetax">What are strategies <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(%20s(X)%20)">/estimators <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(%5Chat%7B%5Ctheta%7D(X)">?</h3>
<p>We want to have our actions be informed on data we observe.</p>
<p>A pure strategy <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s"> is a function from the data space <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathcal%7BX%7D"> to the action space <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A">.</p>
<p>i.e.&nbsp;for a pure strategy, the image of some $x_{i} $ can only be a unique action from <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A">.</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s"> refers to a single strategy and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?S"> refers to a set of strategies.</p>
<p>Steps to calculate <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l(%5Ctheta,%20s)"> for a single strategy across all x’s:</p>
<p>1. Suppose our data space is, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_%7B1%7D,%20x_%7B2%7D"></p>
<p>2. Our action space for strategy s, suppose is, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?a_%7B1%7D,%20a_%7B2%7D"> respectively.</p>
<p>3. Now, we know <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f(x_%7B1%7D%20%7C%20%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D),%20f(x_%7B1%7D%20%7C%20%5Ctheta_%7B2%7D),%20f(x_%7B2%7D%20%7C%20%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D),%20f(x_%7B2%7D%20%7C%20%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D)">. (Say we know the distributions based on observations from the past).</p>
<ol start="4" type="1">
<li>Then <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l(%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D,%20s(x_%7B1%7D))"> = <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l(%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D,%20a_1))"> (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l"> indicates loss associated with an individual action).
<ul>
<li>Similarly for other parameters and actions.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>Let $ s_{1}, s_{2}, s_{3}, s_{4} S $</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout-note callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Note
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>What’s the difference between <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathcal%7BX%7D"> ? <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> is a random variable and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmathcal%7BX%7D"> is the space that contains all <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_i"> a.k.a. the <strong>data space.<br>
<br>
</strong>Since <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> is random, the function <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s(X)"> is also random.<br>
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-note callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Note
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>Why are we interested in calculating <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta,%20s)"> in the form of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?E_%7BX%7D(l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(X))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)">?</p>
<p>Note that, here, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(X))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)"> is the loss incurred when taking the action <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s(X%20=%20x_%7Bi%7D)"> for a fixed <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctheta"><br>
<br>
Then <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?E_%7BX%7D(l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(X))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)"> is the expectation of these losses <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cforall"> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> for a fixed $\theta$</p>
<p>So <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?E_%7BX%7D(l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(X))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)"> helps us summarize (across all <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X">) the loss incurred by a strategy for a <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctheta">.</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AE_%7BX%7D(l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(X))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)%20=%20%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5E%7Bn%7D%20f(x_%7Bi%7D%20%7C%20%5Ctheta).%20l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(x_%7Bi%7D))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)%0A"></p>
<p>Then <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta,%20s)"> = <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?E_%7BX%7D(l(%20%5Ctheta%20,%20s(X))%20%7C%20%5Ctheta)"> asks the question, how well does a strategy perform for a given <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctheta"></p>
<p><br>
However, if calculate <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta,%20s)"> as the median of <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?l(%5Ctheta,%20s(X))"> across all <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> then, we’re only retaining information about one individual <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_%7Bi%7D%20%5Chspace%7B0.3%20em%7D%20%5Cepsilon%20%5Chspace%7B0.3%20em%7D%20X"></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="admissible-strategies" class="level1">
<h1>2. Admissible Strategies</h1>
<div class="callout-important callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Important
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>
<p>A strategy <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s(X)"> is <strong>in</strong>admissible if <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cexists"> another strategy <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s'(X)"> s.t. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta,%20s)%20%3E=%20L(%5Ctheta,%20s')%20%5Cforall%20%5Ctheta"><br>
<br>
With at least one strict inequality.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p><img src="https://www.sowmya-parthiban.com/images/strategies.png" class="img-fluid" width="346" alt="Examples of strategies and losses"> In the above example, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s_3"> is <strong>uniformly worse</strong> than $s_2$, i.e., it is inadmissible because, it <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta_1%20,%20s3)%20%3E%20L(%5Ctheta_1%20,%20s2)"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta_2%20,%20s3)%20%3E%20L(%5Ctheta_2%20,%20s2)"> .</p>
<section id="mixedrandomized-strategies" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="mixedrandomized-strategies">Mixed/Randomized Strategies:</h3>
<p>Going back to our four strategies, we can assign each one a probability. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f(s_%7B1%7D),%20f(s_%7B2%7D)%20..."></p>
<div class="callout-important callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Important
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p><strong>Definition:</strong></p>
<p>A <strong>randomized strategy</strong> is just a weighted sum of our pure strategies based on these probability distributions.</p>
<p>e.g.&nbsp;<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s*%20=%20%5Csum_%7Bi=1%7D%5E%7B4%7Ds_%7Bi%7D.%20f(s_%7Bi%7D)"> is a mixed strategy.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="geometric-representation-of-strategy-space-across-states-of-natureparams" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="geometric-representation-of-strategy-space-across-states-of-natureparams">Geometric Representation of Strategy Space Across States of Nature(Params)</h3>
<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="https://www.sowmya-parthiban.com/images/convex hull.png" class="img-fluid figure-img" width="545"></p>
</figure>
</div>
<p>On the right, we have our four pure strategies and their losses for each <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctheta"> .</p>
<p>On the left</p>
<ul>
<li><p>x-coordinate = <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D,%20s)"></p></li>
<li><p>y-coordinate = <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(%5Ctheta_%7B2%7D,%20s)"></p></li>
<li><p>A <strong>convex hull</strong> around the pink points then is the entire extended (i.e.&nbsp;including mixed) strategy space.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cforall%20%5Chspace%7B0.3%20em%7D%20%5Clambda%5Chspace%7B0.3%20em%7D%20%5Cepsilon%20%5Chspace%7B0.3%20em%7D%20%5B0,1%5D%20:%20%5Chspace%7B0.3%20em%7D%5Clambda%20s_%7B1%7D%20+%20(1-%20%5Clambda)s_%7B2%7D"> is a mixed strategy that falls on the line connecting s1 and s2.</p>
<p>Given, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(s_%7B1%7D,%20%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D)"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?L(s_%7B2%7D,%20%5Ctheta_%7B1%7D)">, and for a given <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda"> s.t. <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s*%20=%20%CE%BBs_1+(1%E2%88%92%CE%BB)s_2">,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AL(s*,%20%5Ctheta_1)%20=%20%5Clambda%20s_%7B1%7D%20+%20(1%20-%20%5Clambda)%20s_%7B2%7D%0A"></p>
<div class="callout-tip callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Tip
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>Heuristically speaking, admissible strategies always fall on the “south-west” border of the convex-hull. These are the strategies that are useful.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="closed-lower-quantant-and-admissibility" class="level4">
<h4 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="closed-lower-quantant-and-admissibility">Closed “Lower Quantant” and Admissibility</h4>
<p><strong>Illustration of a Lower Quantant</strong></p>
<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="https://www.sowmya-parthiban.com/images/lower quantant.png" class="img-fluid figure-img" width="404"></p>
<p></p><figcaption class="figure-caption">The shaded portion here including the red borders here is a closed lower quantant with corner point (x0, y0).</figcaption><p></p>
</figure>
</div>
<div class="callout-important callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Important
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>**Definition:**</p>
<p>A lower quantant whose corner falls on the line x = y is a diagonal lower quantant.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-important callout callout-style-default callout-captioned">
<div class="callout-header d-flex align-content-center">
<div class="callout-icon-container">
<i class="callout-icon"></i>
</div>
<div class="callout-caption-container flex-fill">
Important
</div>
</div>
<div class="callout-body-container callout-body">
<p>**Result:**</p>
<p>A strategy <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s"> is admissible <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ciff"> the closed lower quantant (closed means including the border of the quantant) at s includes no other pt.&nbsp;from S.</p>
</div>
</div>
<p><strong>Illustration of an Inadmissible vs.&nbsp;Admissible Strategy based on the lower quantant</strong></p>
<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="https://www.sowmya-parthiban.com/images/admiss inadmiss.png" class="img-fluid figure-img" width="582"></p>
</figure>
</div>
<p>Suppose the purple circle here is a convex hull of strategies <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?S">. The lower quantant with its corner at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s'"> clearly includes other pts in <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?S">, hence, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s'"> is inadmissible. The same is not true for <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?s">.</p>
</section>
</section>
